Before reading this chapter, I often connected participation with caring. When someone gets involved, it seems to show that they care enough to act. Participation also gives me a sense that my presence matters. Part of that meaning comes from what I learn or feel, but another part comes from being able to affect the outcome. Bishop’s discussion made me question whether having an impact always means that our participation is meaningful in the way we intended.

The Futurist performances challenged my understanding because even the audience’s negative reactions could support the artists’ goals. People could disagree with Futurism, but their anger and violent responses still helped create the atmosphere the artists wanted. This made me think about how we sometimes act before understanding what we are joining. Caring can be a reason to get involved, but it does not guarantee that our involvement will help. Our actions might support something we disagree with or cause harm we did not expect.

The discussion of Paris Dada raised another question for me. Over time, audiences became familiar with the performances. They came prepared to interrupt and provoke the artists. What was supposed to challenge expectations became something people expected. The artists and audience seemed to know their roles: one side would provoke, and the other would respond.

I do not think this means the audience’s reactions were fake or that the events had no impact. However, I wonder whether participation can become a habit that no longer asks us to think. We might feel involved because we are doing something, while repeating a response we already know.

For me, this is also a question about the artist’s responsibility. If artists keep receiving the same reactions, how can they recognize when their expression has become a routine? They might also start repeating themselves because they know what will get a response. I am interested in how an artwork can leave room for people to pause, question, or disagree in unexpected ways. After this reading, I want to pay more attention to what participation makes possible, beyond the fact that people joined.
